faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour. Agents leave a workshop keen and are back to writing listings the old way inside a fortnight, because nobody built the habit on live stock. Our program runs across three months on your own listings, so by the end it is simply how the office writes — and ongoing support keeps it current as the tools move."
  - q: "Can this be rolled out across a franchise network?"
    a: "Yes, and it's the most efficient way to buy it. The program runs the same for one office or twenty, and a network gets a shared standard for voice, review and compliance rather than each office improvising. Ask us about multi-office pricing."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement, so you know the total before you start. Tell us how many agents and property managers you'd send and we'll give you the number, along with what we'd expect to compress first."
  - q: "Will it replace our copywriter?"
    a: "It changes what they spend the day on. Agencies that keep a copywriter move them up the chain — campaign strategy, the flagship listings, owning the review standard — rather than producing forty listings a month by hand."
  - q: "Do agents actually keep using it after the training?"
    a: "That's the reason the program runs over three months rather than as a one-day course. People build the workflows on their own live listings during the program, so by the end it is already how they work, and the ongoing support keeps it that way."
  - q: "What about property management, not just sales?"
    a: "Property management usually sees the faster return. The correspondence volume is higher, more of it is routine, and the emotional cost of writing arrears and maintenance messages all day is real. We cover both sides in the same program."
  - q: "Is our vendor and buyer data safe?"
    a: "With the right tools set up properly, and that setup is part of the training — which tools carry business terms, what should never be pasted in, and how to keep client details out of anything that trains on your inputs."
